a11bd2b2-24f1-40d5-a92f-920fdab90cb9	36f398d4-d44f-4447-a691-6e47f4b457b9	791553e4-9051-47cc-84c5-ef11694ee801	3	 main platforms active in the European market are:\n TimoCom (TC Truck & Cargo):\nThe largest European load board with over 750,000 transport offers daily. Available in 44 countries.\n Transopedia:\nStrong presence in Eastern Europe. Good for Poland, Romania, and Balkan freight.\n Teleroute:\nOne of the oldest European platforms. Strong in France, Belgium, and Iberia.\n Bursa de Freight (BestMile, Nolis):\nWidely used in Romania and surrounding markets.\n Convoy / Uber Freight:\nDigital freight brokerages expanding into European markets with automated tendering.\n\n\n4. European Regulations Every Dispatcher Must Know\n4.1 Drivers' Hours and Rest Rules (EU Regulation 561/2006)\nEU Regulation 561/2006 governs driving hours for all commercial vehicles over 3.5 tonnes in EU member states. This is one of the most important regulations for load planning, as it directly determines how far a driver can travel and when they must rest.\n\nRule\nRequirement\nDaily driving limit\n9 hours (extendable to 10 hours twice per week)\nWeekly driving limit\n56 hours maximum\nFortnightly (2-week) limit\n90 hours maximum across any two consecutive weeks\nDaily rest\nMinimum 11 hours (reducible to 9 hours up to 3 times between weekly rests)\nBreak requirement\n45-minute break after 4.5 hours of driving (can be split: 15 + 30 min)\nWeekly rest\nMinimum 45 hours (regular) or 24 hours (reduced, must be compensated)\nRegular weekly rest location\nMust be taken outside the cab since January 2022\n\n?  When planning multi-day loads, always build in the driver's rest requirements before estimating ETA. A realistic route plan that accounts for breaks will always outperform an optimistic one that ignores them.\n\n4.2 Weekend and Holiday Driving Bans\nMost European countries impose driving bans on trucks over a certain weight during weekends, public holidays, and specific peak perio