ccee5342-1032-4c93-87a7-df7f815fd5d7	59050f63-61ce-4125-9dc1-8c1b20f09f54	7ba430b5-849e-48e7-aba9-902af6db4577	7	 roads, regardless of nationality.\n Calculated based on: total distance driven in Switzerland × GVW × emission class factor.\n The LSVA is collected automatically via satellite tracking or tachograph data at border crossing.\n Night driving ban 22:0005:00 and weekend ban apply in addition to the LSVA fee.\n Tunnel restrictions: Gotthard and San Bernardino tunnels have queue management systems  dispatchers should monitor wait times and consider rail alternatives (RoLa piggyback transport).\n\n\n6. Load Planning Fundamentals\n6.1 Understanding Load Types in European Freight\nEuropean dispatchers regularly work with several distinct freight categories, each requiring different planning approaches:\n\nFull Truck Load (FTL / FTL)\nA single shipment fills the entire trailer. This is the most common load type for dispatchers. The customer pays for the full trailer capacity regardless of how much space is used. Planning advantages include direct routing, no multiple stops, and simpler documentation.\n\nLess-than-Truckload (LTL / Groupage / Consolidation)\nMultiple smaller shipments from different customers are combined into one trailer. More complex planning is required because:\n Loading sequence must match delivery order (last delivery loaded last, first delivery loaded first).\n Weight distribution must remain within axle limits even as cargo is unloaded at interim stops.\n Multiple CMR waybills are issued  one per consignment.\n Hub-and-spoke networks are used by major groupage operators (DB Schenker, DHL Freight, Dachser, Kuehne+Nagel).\n\nTemperature-Controlled (Reefer / ATP)\nRefrigerated trailers carry perishable goods (food, pharmaceuticals, chemicals). Key planning requirements:\n ATP certification required for international temperature-controlled transport (ATP Convention).\n Temperature ranges: chilled (+2°C to +8°C), frozen (-18°C or below), multi-temperature.\n Pre-cooling time must be factored into pickup scheduling  typically 24 hours before loading.\n Continuous temperature logging required. Any deviation must be documented and communicated immediately.\n Loading and unloading must happen quickly to avoid temperature excursions  coordinate precise appointment times.\n\nHazardous Materials (ADR)\nTransport of dangerous goods across Europe is governed by the ADR Agreement (European Agreement concerning the International Carriage of Dangerous Goods by Road). As a dispatcher:\n You must	500	[-0.025512695,-0.011375427,-0.01247406,0.02999878,-0.022445679,-0.013587952,-0.0025234222,0.0234375,-0.04437256,-0.020050049,0.051574707,-0.002281189,-0.013519287,-0.007675171,-0.022857666,-0.00047183037,-0.027420044,-0.01828003,0.013000488,-0.01876831,0.03668213,0.03100586,-0.023605347,-0.013084412,-0.02142334,-0.0056915283,0.04675293,0.015838623,-0.018814087,0.014579773,0.020584106,0.048217773,-0.024719238,-0.042022705,0.036621094,-0.030212402,0.041107178,0.03665161,-0.003288269,0.04284668,0.024719238,-0.0034713745,-0.030258179,0.055541992,-0.006767273,-0.011695862,-0.040893555,0.038848877,-0.016784668,-0.018981934,-0.059814453,-0.05783081,-0.029388428,-0.0039749146,0.046173096,0.009963989,0.0071792603,0.12719727,-0.023147583,0.019699097,-0.0149002075,-0.02859497,-0.0149383545,-0.023803711,-0.041107178,0.044158936,0.028549194,0.040222168,-0.038635254,-0.01524353,-0.06561279,0.021881104,0.0015964508,0.013320923,0.004753113,-0.0014791489,0.006099701,0.051208496,0.030197144,0.020904541,0.018875122,0.03189087,-0.0038585663,0.0048294067,-0.0029125214,0.025970459,-0.059661865,-0.08630371,0.023910522,0.009284973,0.022079468,0.0054397583,0.0041122437,-0.02709961,0.031677246,0.030410767,0.014579773,-0.0074424744,0.028701782,-0.0146484375,-0.005580902,-0.0053215027,0.005168915,0.02458191,0.04083252,-0.036224365,0.02293396,0.0066833496,-0.015403748,0.084350586,0.015792847,-0.003921509,0.018753052,0.03970337,-0.00