2ead89d1-c74c-47d6-98c4-fdc70e049f89	c5021bcd-8030-4b72-8101-82ff232ff7c0	7ba430b5-849e-48e7-aba9-902af6db4577	5	 Confirmation\nDispatcher\nGood morning, this is Alex from FastFreight Logistics. I'm calling to confirm your collection scheduled for today at 10:00 from your Munich warehouse. Driver Vasile will be with you  his number is +40 721 444 333. He's currently en route, ETA to you is 09:45. Is there anything specific he needs at the gate?\n\nScenario B  Delay Notification\nDispatcher\nGood afternoon, I'm calling from FastFreight about your delivery due today at 14:00. I have an update  our driver is currently delayed at the German-Swiss border due to higher than usual crossing times. He is in the queue and we expect him to be through within 90 minutes. Revised ETA to you is 16:00. I will send you a written confirmation now and update you again at 15:30. I apologise for the inconvenience.\n\nScenario C  Damage or Exception Reporting\nDispatcher\nGood morning. I need to inform you of an issue with today's delivery. Upon arrival at the receiver in Lyon, our driver discovered that 3 pallets in the consignment show external moisture damage. The driver has noted this on the CMR and taken photographic evidence. We have already contacted the receiver. I will send you the photos and the noted CMR within the hour. Please let me know how you would like to proceed and we will fully support your claim process.\n\n4.3 Email Communication Standards\nEmail is used for confirmations, documentation, and formal communication. Apply these standards to every customer-facing email:\n Subject line: Always include load reference number. Example: 'Delivery Confirmation  Load Ref FL-2024-8823 | Stuttgart 14 Nov'\n Opening: Address by name. 'Dear Mr. Fischer' for formal relationships; 'Hi Laura' for established contacts once appropriate.\n Body structure: State purpose in first sentence. Use short paragraphs or numbered points  never walls of text.\n Closing: Always confirm ne