ace197da-4ae8-4f11-8ba8-5c8ce3d84465	c5021bcd-8030-4b72-8101-82ff232ff7c0	7ba430b5-849e-48e7-aba9-902af6db4577	8	1\nAcknowledge\n'I understand, and I'm sorry to hear this has happened.'\n2\nListen fully\nLet the person finish completely before responding. Do not interrupt.\n3\nTake ownership\n'Let me look into this right now and come back to you within 15 minutes.'\n4\nFollow up\nCall back within the promised timeframe  even if you don't yet have the full answer.\n\n6.2 Handling an Angry Driver\nDriver frustration is often a symptom of feeling unsupported or undervalued. Responding with defensiveness escalates the situation. Responding with calm acknowledgement usually de-escalates it quickly.\n\nExample  Driver Angry About Waiting Time at Receiver\nDriver (angry)\nI've been here for FOUR hours! Nobody is unloading me! This is a joke! I'm missing my rest time!\nDispatcher ?\nI don't know what you want me to do about it, it's the receiver's fault.\nDispatcher ?\nI hear you, Bogdan  four hours is unacceptable and I understand you're frustrated. I'm calling the receiver right now. While I have them on the line, please stay parked and I'll update you in five minutes. Your waiting time will be fully documented for compensation. I'm on it.\n\n6.3 Delivering Bad News to a Customer\nWhen something goes wrong  a load is late, cargo is damaged, or a driver cannot make a collection  the way you communicate the news matters as much as the news itself. Follow this principle:\n Lead with the fact, not the cause. Customers want to know what is happening before they want to know why.\n Follow immediately with your action. What are you doing right now to resolve it?\n Offer a realistic new timeline. Do not over-promise to soften the blow. Under-promise and over-deliver.\n Express genuine accountability. 'I'm sorry this has happened' is not a legal admission of liability  it is basic human courtesy and it matters.\n\nBad News Call  Structure\nDispatcher\n[Fact] Unfortunate