9f3a7ed7-63c5-459b-ae1a-0d55e76ccbe0	c5021bcd-8030-4b72-8101-82ff232ff7c0	7ba430b5-849e-48e7-aba9-902af6db4577	9	 window today.\nDispatcher\n[Action] I have already contacted roadside assistance  they are en route with an ETA of 45 minutes to the driver's location.\nDispatcher\n[New timeline] Depending on repair time, realistically your cargo arrives between 17:00 and 18:00 today. I will call you the moment the driver is moving again with a confirmed ETA.\nDispatcher\n[Accountability] I'm sorry for the disruption this causes. Is there anything we can do on our end to help manage the impact on your side?\n\n\n7\nEmergency and Crisis Communication\n\nEmergencies happen in trucking  breakdowns, accidents, cargo incidents, medical emergencies involving drivers, and extreme weather events. Your ability to communicate calmly and systematically in these moments is critical to safety, legal compliance, and operational recovery.\n\n7.1 The First 5 Minutes of an Emergency\nWhen a driver calls with an emergency, resist the instinct to react emotionally. Follow this sequence:\n\nMin\nAction\n01\nLISTEN  let the driver describe the situation without interrupting. Confirm you can hear them clearly. Note: location, nature of the incident, driver condition.\n12\nSECURE  confirm driver is safe and vehicle is in a safe position. If not: emergency services first, everything else second. European emergency number: 112.\n23\nASSESS  ask: Is anyone injured? Is cargo secure? Is the vehicle blocking traffic? Is there a fire or fuel spill risk?\n34\nNOTIFY  call your operations manager immediately. Do not wait until you have full information  call with what you know.\n45\nCOORDINATE  begin contacting roadside assistance, insurance, customer. Keep the driver on standby and call back every 1015 minutes with updates.\n\n7.2 Emergency Contacts Every Dispatcher Must Have Ready\n European emergency services: 112 (universal EU number for police, fire, ambulance)\n Company roadside 