4c9a79a6-ba59-4d6a-8f35-b6785e6fef97	c5021bcd-8030-4b72-8101-82ff232ff7c0	7ba430b5-849e-48e7-aba9-902af6db4577	10	 duty manager: Always have an out-of-hours number for escalation\n Country-specific breakdown providers: ADAC (Germany), AA/RAC (UK), ÖAMTC (Austria), PZM (Poland), ACR (Romania)\n\n7.3 Accident Reporting Communication\nIf a driver is involved in a road traffic accident, the communication sequence is critical for legal and insurance purposes:\n Step 1: Driver calls 112 and ensures safety at scene (warning triangles, hazard lights, stay away from carriageway)\n Step 2: Driver calls dispatcher with: location (GPS coordinate or nearest km marker), number of vehicles involved, any injuries, whether police have been called\n Step 3: Dispatcher calls operations manager and insurance company immediately\n Step 4: Driver must NOT move the vehicle until police arrive and assess (in most EU countries)\n Step 5: Driver completes European Accident Report Form (available in most company vehicle packs)  one for each vehicle involved\n Step 6: Dispatcher notifies customer of potential delivery impact\n Step 7: Dispatcher logs full timeline of events in TMS with timestamps\n\n?  CAUTION\nDrivers must never admit fault at an accident scene, even if they believe they were at fault. This is a matter for insurance companies and legal representatives. Instruct drivers on this policy during their onboarding and reinforce it regularly.\n\n\n8\nWritten Communication and TMS Documentation\n\nWritten communication in dispatching serves two purposes: it ensures clarity in the moment, and it creates a record that can be referenced days, weeks, or months later in the event of disputes, claims, or audits. Every experienced dispatcher treats their TMS notes as a legal document  because in some cases, they are.\n\n8.1 TMS Notes  What to Record and When\nEvent\nWhat to Record in TMS\nDriver departure\nActual departure time, tachograph start confirmed, any pre-departure issues