77de4cd6-771a-4173-a2d3-77df759ecf21	896d0dc9-2bb8-4e06-a2c2-4f8804d8f788	2	Contraindications and Warnings	Explore the contraindications for Paracetamol use and important warnings to ensure safe consumption.	Contraindications,Allergic reactions,Liver damage,Drug interactions	2	## Contraindications and Warnings\n\n### Product Overview\n\n**Paracetamol 500mg Tablets**  \n- **Active Substance**: Paracetamol (acetaminophen) 500mg per tablet  \n- **Manufacturer**: TestPharma Ltd  \n- **Batch Standard**: BP/USP compliant  \n\nParacetamol is widely used for the relief of mild to moderate pain and fever. It is effective for conditions such as headaches, muscle aches, and cold symptoms. However, safe use requires adherence to specific contraindications and warnings.\n\n---\n\n### Indications for Use\n\nParacetamol is indicated for the relief of:  \n- Headache and migraine  \n- Toothache  \n- Sore throat  \n- Period pain  \n- Muscle aches and joint pain  \n- Cold and flu symptoms  \n- Fever (high temperature)  \n\n---\n\n### Dosage and Administration\n\n#### Adults and Children Over 16 Years  \n- **Dose**: 1–2 tablets every 4–6 hours as needed  \n- **Maximum Dose**: 2 tablets per dose  \n- **Daily Limit**: 8 tablets (4000mg) in a 24-hour period  \n- **Interval**: Leave at least 4 hours between doses  \n\n#### Children 12 to 15 Years  \n- **Dose**: 1 tablet every 4–6 hours as needed  \n- **Daily Limit**: 4 tablets (2000mg) in a 24-hour period  \n\n#### Children Under 12 Years  \n- **Do Not Use**: Paracetamol 500mg tablets are not suitable for children under 12 years.  \n- **Alternative**: Use age-appropriate paracetamol liquid or lower-strength formulations.  \n\n---\n\n### Contraindications\n\nDo **NOT** take Paracetamol 500mg tablets if you:  \n- Are allergic to paracetamol or any other ingredient in the product.  \n- Have severe liver disease.  \n- Have severe kidney disease.  \n- Are already taking other products containing paracetamol.  \n\n---\n\n### Warnings\n\n- **Dosage Limits**: Do not exceed the stated dose.  \n- **Overdose Risks**: Taking more than the recommended dose can cause serious liver damage, which may not be immediately apparent.  \n  - If an overdose occurs, seek emergency medical attention immediately—even if you feel well.  \n- **Drug Interactions**: Avoid taking paracetamol with other paracetamol-containing products.  \n- **Consult a Doctor**: Speak with a healthcare professional before use if you are taking warfarin (a blood-thinning medicine).  \n\n---\n\n### Side Effects\n\nParacetamol is generally well tolerated when taken at recommended doses. However, some rare and very rare side effects may occur:  \n\n| **Side Effect**                | **Frequency**                  | **Details**                                                                 |\n|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Skin rash or allergic reaction | Rare (up to 1 in 1000 people) | May include itching, redness, or swelling.                                 |\n| Low blood pressure             | Rare (up to 1 in 1000 people) | Applicable only if given by injection (not relevant for tablets).          |\n| Blood disorders                | Very rare                     | Includes conditions like thrombocytopenia (low platelet count).            |\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Always follow the dosage guidelines to avoid overdose and potential liver damage.  \n- Be aware of contraindications, especially if you have liver or kidney disease or are allergic to paracetamol.  \n- Consult a healthcare provider if you are taking other medications, such as warfarin.  \n- Monitor for rare side effects, and seek medical advice if you experience symptoms like rash or allergic reactions.  \n\nBy adhering to these contraindications and warnings, you can ensure the safe and effective use of Paracetamol 500mg tablets.
e1c1581b-4ef1-4d6d-8939-c62402367cee	896d0dc9-2bb8-4e06-a2c2-4f8804d8f788	4	Overdose Information and Legal Category	Understand the risks of overdose, symptoms to watch for, and legal classification of Paracetamol 500mg Tablets.	Overdose symptoms,Emergency response,Antidote,Legal category	4	## Overdose Information and Legal Category\n\n### Overdose Information\n\nParacetamol overdose is a serious medical emergency. It can lead to severe health complications, including liver damage. Understanding the symptoms and appropriate response is critical.\n\n#### Symptoms of Overdose\n- **Early symptoms**: Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain.\n- **Delayed symptoms**: Liver damage may occur 24 to 72 hours after overdose, often without initial symptoms.\n\n#### Emergency Response\nIf an overdose is suspected:\n1. Seek emergency medical attention immediately.\n2. Inform medical personnel about the suspected overdose.\n\n#### Antidote\n- **N-acetylcysteine (NAC)** is the antidote for paracetamol overdose.\n- NAC is most effective when administered within **8 to 10 hours** of ingestion.\n\n---\n\n### Legal Category\n\nParacetamol 500mg Tablets are classified under the **General Sale List (GSL)**. This means they are available without a prescription.\n\n#### Pack Sizes\n- **16 tablets**\n- **32 tablets**\n\n---\n\n### Storage Instructions\n\nProper storage ensures the safety and effectiveness of Paracetamol 500mg Tablets. Follow these guidelines:\n\n- Store below **25°C** in a dry place.\n- Keep out of direct sunlight.\n- Keep out of the reach and sight of children.\n- Do not use after the expiry date printed on the packaging.\n- Avoid storing in a bathroom or near a sink.\n\n---\n\n### Side Effects\n\nWhile rare, side effects from Paracetamol 500mg Tablets may occur. Monitor for the following:\n\n#### Rare Side Effects (may affect up to 1 in 1,000 people):\n- Skin rash or allergic reaction.\n- Low blood pressure (applicable only if given by injection, not tablets).\n\n#### Very Rare Side Effects:\n- Blood disorders, such as thrombocytopenia (low platelet count).\n- Abnormal liver function tests with prolonged high-dose use.\n\nIf you notice any side effects not listed here, contact your doctor or pharmacist immediately.
